===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} To [[detain]] (a person) in [[conversation]] against their will.
#: {{syn|en|accost|waylay}}
#: {{cot|en|grab by the lapels}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Tramp Abroad|chapter=26|passage=He backed Mr. Lykins against an iron fence, '''buttonholed''' him, fastened him with his eye, like {{w|The Rime of the Ancient Mariner|the Ancient Mariner}}, and proceeded to unfold his narrative as placidly and peacefully as if we were all stretched comfortably in a blossomy summer meadow instead of being persecuted by a wintry midnight tempest:}}
#* {{RQ:Mitchell Gone with the Wind|chapter=50|page=890|passage=He '''buttonholed''' people on the street and related details of his child’s miraculous progress without even prefacing his remarks with the hypocritical but polite: “I know everyone thinks their own child is smart but—”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Here they are, the brainless few we had been raised to pity and fear, the Stone Age oafs and the seething runts and the ominous, swaggering weightlifters, '''buttonholing''' kids like me out on Chancellor Avenue and telling us to keep our baseball bats at the ready in case we were called in the night to take to the streets [...]|author=w:Philip Roth|title=w:The Plot Against America|year=2004}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To cut one or more buttonholes (in).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[sew]] by [[buttonhole stitch]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|surgery|mostly|_|archaic}} To make a small slot-like [[incision]] in (intentionally or unintentionally).
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To apply a [[flowery]] [[formation]] in.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1871-12-17|volume=28|issue=25|title=Forestry and Apiculture|url=https://archive.org/details/americanbeejourn28hami/page/781?q=premious|author=Henry K. Staley|work=[https://americanbeejournal.com/ The American Bee Journal]|page=781b of 779a–782b|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_american-bee-journal_1891-12-17_28_25/page/781?q=premious|passage=What tree of more pulchritude and symmetricalness could you nominate for roadside, street and public park planting than the graceful linden? We should possess more of the spirit which predominates in this direction in the crowning gem of America’s constitutionality—the City of Washington, D. C. ‘‘{{w|Unter den Linden|Unter den Lindens}}’’ would not only be found in Germany, but in all the large cities of our grand and glorious United States. Washington has her Unter den Linden in the Massachussetts{{sic}} avenue. Her North and South Capital streets are '''buttonholed''' with the stately tulips, which are fit emblems to thus adorn the meridian of the United States.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic|rare}} To [[attain]] buttonhole-[[like]] [[formation]]s on cutting.
#* {{quote-book|year=1927|en|author=w:Warwick Deeping|title={{w|Doomsday (novel)|Doomsday}}|page=77|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.206293/page/n85|passage=Furze cut it himself after his dinner, with great care and concentrated solemnity, finding the loaf rather too new and the knife too blunt for the carving of slices of ideal thinness. The beastly things would '''buttonhole!'''}}

